-## Draft decision D105
+## Draft decision D105 (revised after R1)
 
-1. Equivalence band for Z<=6: epsilon = 0.03, i.e. W(Z<=6) must lie in (0.47, 0.53). Reason: the pilot value and the Z<=6 control values lie within 0.497–0.508, so 0.03 leaves a margin for probe-induced shifts of that size, while a W-AUC of 0.53 is substantively negligible next to the prefix's value near 1.0.
-2. Minimum difference: Delta must exceed 0.25 (one-sided lower bound), half the distance between chance (0.5) and perfect within-pair ranking (1.0).
-3. Causal decision rule: the generator's level-7 code choice counts as sensitive to the candidate directions only if all three hold: the one-sided lower bound of the Q directed effect exceeds 0.01 (TV); the lower bound of Q minus the high-variance control exceeds 0; the lower bound of Q minus the shuffled-digit control exceeds 0.
-4. Error rates: three primary tests (Z<=6 equivalence, Delta, causal), family-wise alpha = 0.05 with Bonferroni, alpha = 0.05/3 per test, as in plan v3.7; intervals accordingly (two-sided 1 − 2 alpha for the equivalence, one-sided 1 − alpha bounds otherwise).
-5. TEST size: 500 pairs (1,000 puzzles) from seed namespace 10. Reason: the primary tests need far fewer pairs (to be confirmed by the final simulation), but the secondary strata need more: code-different pairs are about 65%, pairs with exactly one solved member about 20% (about 100 at 500 pairs) for the rescue analysis. Estimated cost: extraction about 1 h, causal evaluation about 1 h, probe scoring minutes.
-6. The final simulation (after these numbers are fixed) resamples the pilot per-pair values (PROBE-VAL pairs) with the real decision rules, the interval switch at the bounds and the Bonferroni levels, under the pilot effects and under boundary scenarios (W(Z<=6) shifted to 0.5 ± epsilon, Delta shifted to 0.25, causal effects shifted to the thresholds), and reports the joint power and the false-positive rates. If the joint power at 500 pairs is below 0.9, the protocol revisits the size before TEST generation.
+1. Claims and error control. Two confirmatory claims, each at alpha = 0.025 (family-wise 0.05 over the two claims): (A) the state reading (the candidate mask is readable within pairs from the prefix and not from Z<=6), and (B) the causal reading (the level-7 code distribution moves towards the full-swap reference under the candidate-subspace patch beyond the named controls). Each claim is a conjunction of conditions; by the intersection-union principle every condition is tested at the full 0.025 and the claim holds only if all its conditions hold. Conditions are not reported as separate confirmatory results.
+2. Decision bounds. Every decision uses a finite-sample valid one-sided confidence bound for the mean of a bounded per-pair quantity: the betting confidence bound of Waudby-Smith and Ramdas (2024, "Estimating means of bounded random variables by betting", JRSSB), hedged-capital form with predictable plug-in bets, at level 0.025 per side, with the known range of each quantity (per-pair W-AUC in [0, 1]; Delta and the causal contrasts in [−1, 1], rescaled to [0, 1]). This replaces the D101 pair-level bootstrap and its switch to exact bounds at the ceiling: one rule for every case, no data-dependent branch. Pair-level bootstrap intervals are reported for estimation only. The implementation is fixed in code before TEST and checked by simulation for coverage on synthetic bounded data, including degenerate distributions (all values 1.0, many ties at 0.5). The preregistration promises the error level only for this procedure; the pilot-to-TEST probe heads are fitted on PROBE-TRAIN and frozen, so inference is conditional on the frozen heads.
+3. Claim A conditions. (A1) Equivalence of Z<=6: the lower and the upper one-sided bounds of W(Z<=6) both lie strictly inside (0.5 − epsilon, 0.5 + epsilon) with epsilon = 0.03. Centre 0.5: the tie-scored W-AUC of a ranking that does not discriminate candidate from non-candidate members, not an empirical control mean (D101: the E3 controls are diagnostics). Justification of 0.03 independent of the expected result: a pair-weighted excess of 3 net correct orderings per 100 discordant comparisons is treated as negligible next to readouts that order nearly all of them; forced ties from locally identical Z<=6 states count as ties, as in every W-AUC. (A2) Minimum difference: the lower one-sided bound of Delta exceeds 0.25. Justification: a large effect on the W-AUC scale; with W(Z<=6) near 0.5 it requires the prefix to order at least about three quarters of the discordant comparisons correctly; it is not derived from the pilot value.
+4. Claim B conditions (D102(7) kept, with the D104 control). The lower one-sided bound exceeds the threshold for each of: (B1) the Q directed effect, threshold 0.01, i.e. an average movement of one percentage point of total variation towards the reference; recipients whose reference distance is below 0.01 stay in the mean, and the heterogeneity across recipients is reported descriptively; (B2) Q minus the mean of the norm-matched random subspaces, threshold 0; (B3) Q minus the high-variance control, threshold 0; (B4) Q minus the shuffled-digit control, threshold 0. The claim is limited to movement of the level-7 code distribution towards the reference relative to these controls under this intervention; no mechanistic digit-specific claim is made, because the controls are matched in norm but not in alignment with the full difference.
+5. TEST size and scope. N = max(N_power, 250) pairs, rounded up to a multiple of 50, from seed namespace 10, where N_power is the smallest N at which the simulation of item 6 gives a joint power of at least 0.9 for each claim under the pilot model; 250 is the PROBE-VAL size, the floor for the descriptive secondary analyses (code-different stratum, inference-wave depth, level-7 query readout). No precision target is committed for secondary strata; they are reported with intervals. The full-generation causal variant and the rescue/harm analysis are not part of TEST (exploratory, later, if at all). N is fixed before TEST generation and never changed after TEST outcomes.
+6. Simulation (before TEST generation; its code and seed fixed with the protocol). The generating model resamples whole per-pair outcome vectors from the PROBE-VAL pilot (W(P), W(Z), the causal per-pair contrasts for both orders averaged), so forced ties, ranges and the identity Delta = W(P) − W(Z) are kept. Scenarios: (a) the pilot model, for power; (b) weaker alternatives justified in advance (W(P) lowered towards 0.85, causal Q effect halved), for power; (c) null boundaries where exactly one condition is false and the others hold (W(Z) at 0.5 ± epsilon via mixture with pairs from a shifted component that keeps per-pair values in range; Delta at 0.25; each causal contrast at its threshold), for the false-positive rate of each condition and claim. Monte Carlo standard errors are reported. The pilot heads were selected on PROBE-VAL, so the simulation is a planning model, not an independent calibration.
+7. Procedure. The TEST run uses a separate frozen TEST runner, not the pilot scripts: frozen head and control identifiers (SHA-256), pair ids, the exclusion log (an orbit collision replaces only the new candidate pair), verified-identical inputs scored as ties, a stop on any non-finite value, no silent reduction of the denominator; the bound implementation and tie handling are tested on synthetic data before TEST generation.
 
-## Open questions for the review
+## Review
 
-- Are epsilon = 0.03 and the Delta minimum 0.25 defensible as substantively meaningful, given that the PROBE-VAL controls of the prefix representations showed systematic W-AUC shifts up to ±0.07 (D101 observation 3) while the Z<=6 controls shifted by at most 0.008?
-- Is a Bonferroni split over three tests appropriate when the state reading requires two of them jointly (equivalence and Delta) and the causal test answers a different question?
-- Is the causal minimum effect 0.01 TV sensible relative to the pilot (Q 0.052; full-swap reference 0.061; 14.2% of recipients with TV(clean, full) below 0.01)?
-- Is 500 pairs justified, or is a smaller TEST preferable, given that larger TEST sets make small probe-induced biases in W(Z<=6) more likely to exceed a narrow band?
